    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'll start with the solenoids. Honestly though, sometimes these things feel almost random, like something is just messing with the sensors or the flow for no reason. Is there anything else that could cause that kind of intermittent, \"ghost\" behavior in the hydraulic circuit that isn't just a part clearly wearing out?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Ghost\" behavior in a hydraulic circuit, especially if it seems random, often points to a subtle system-wide issue rather than a single, obvious broken part. Consider a failing pressure sensor that is sending erratic data to the TCM, making the computer think the pressure needs to spike when it doesn't. Another possibility is a low-level electrical ground issue somewhere in the harness, which can cause intermittent signals to fluctuate wildly. Sometimes, even a tiny air bubble trapped in the hydraulic lines can cause unpredictable pressure fluctuations under load.",
      "source": "target",
